                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Define the relevant market`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The whole case turns on market definition. The acquirer pushes the <strong>broad</strong> “all edible oils” market, where the merged share is only ~22% and HHI barely moves — case closed, they'd say. Complainant retailers push the <strong>narrow</strong> “branded sunflower + soyabean refined oils,” where the overlap is alarming and shares soar. Run the <strong>SSNIP / hypothetical-monopolist test</strong> on the switching surveys and diversion data: would a monopolist of just those two oils profitably raise price 5–10%? <em>Define it broadly and harm vanishes by assumption; define it narrowly and the case is real.</em> Flag which states are separate geographic markets.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Measure concentration & screen for harm`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Compute the <strong>HHI</strong> and the <strong>HHI delta</strong> pre- and post-merger: national sunflower oil rises ~1,950 → ~2,920, a <strong>+970 delta</strong> against the +100/+150 review thresholds, and three states exceed HHI 3,000. Apply the structural presumption — but a high HHI is a <em>trigger that shifts the burden to efficiencies and entry, not a verdict</em>. The NCLAT will demand more than shares. Classify each candidate market (national + three states) as safe / needs-scrutiny / presumptively harmful, and state whether the harm is <strong>unilateral</strong> (the merged firm alone raises price) or <strong>coordinated</strong> (fewer firms collude more easily).`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Estimate the price effect`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Turn assertion into a number. With a high <strong>diversion ratio</strong> between Aarav and Sundara, estimate the <strong>upward pricing pressure (UPP / GUPPI)</strong> and run a simplified merger simulation — switching surveys put diversion around 35–45%, which maps to the evidenced <strong>6–11%</strong> price rise. Then discipline the acquirer's <strong>₹900 crore synergies</strong> on the three-part standard: are they <em>merger-specific, verifiable, and passed-through</em> to shoppers? Credit only what survives all three, then net the credited pass-through against the ₹1,400–2,600 crore welfare loss. An estimate the appellate body accepts beats both a hunch and an elegant model that overshoots.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recommend: approve, block, or remedy`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Convert the analysis into an order the Commission can defend. <strong>Unconditional clearance</strong> if the effects are de minimis; <strong>prohibition</strong> if no remedy cures the harm; <strong>conditional clearance with remedies</strong> otherwise. A <strong>structural divestiture</strong> of the overlapping sunflower brand to a credible buyer restores competition once and lets the atta business and clean geographies proceed; a <strong>behavioural price cap</strong> is hard to monitor across thousands of SKUs <em>and you'll babysit it for years</em>. Blocking the whole ₹14,200 crore deal when harm is confined to one segment is disproportionate and invites a successful appeal. Weigh Type I against Type II error — and decide <em>today</em>.`,

