                    coach_default:          `Finding logged.`,
                    coach_r1_broad:         `<strong>You adopted the parties' “all edible oils” market — where the merged share is 22% and HHI barely moves.</strong> But retailers swear shoppers treat sunflower and soyabean refined oils as a distinct set and won't switch to mustard or palm on a 7% rise. You've made the harm vanish by assumption. Did you run the test, or accept the framing?`,
                    coach_r1_single:        `<strong>A single-brand market is too narrow to be a market at all.</strong> Antitrust markets are defined by demand substitution among rival products, not around one firm's own SKUs. The NCLAT will discard this as gerrymandered — go back to the SSNIP logic.`,
                    coach_r1_global:        `<strong>A “global commodity” market is the acquirer's dream and the shopper's nightmare.</strong> Crude oil trades globally, but Indian consumers buy a branded refined product through modern trade — they cannot import their cooking oil from an exchange. That definition is indefensible.`,
                    coach_r1_accept:        `<strong>Accepting the parties' framing without testing it is Common Error number one.</strong> Market definition is contestable and outcome-determinative — the entire case can flip on this one decision. A hypothetical-monopolist test on the switching data is exactly what an appellate body will look for.`,
                    coach_r1_tested:        `<strong>That is how you anchor a defensible case.</strong> A narrow product market, separate state geographies, and a SSNIP test run on your own switching and diversion data — not the parties' slide. The shares and HHI that follow now rest on tested foundations, not assertion.`,
                    coach_r2_verdict:       `<strong>Post-merger HHI is 2,920 with a +970 delta — presumptively concerning, agreed.</strong> But the parties will tell the Commission, and the NCLAT on appeal, that high shares don't prove higher prices. Where is your estimate of <em>how much</em> price rises? A presumption gets you to scrutiny, not to a defensible order.`,
                    coach_r2_dismiss:       `<strong>Dismissing the screen because “shares don't prove prices” throws away the structural presumption entirely.</strong> The breach is real and it shifts the burden onto the parties. Use it as the trigger it is, then go and estimate the effect — don't wave it away.`,
                    coach_r2_coordinated:   `<strong>A coordinated-effects-only theory understates the case.</strong> The distinguishing fact here is that Aarav and Sundara are each other's closest substitute — that is a textbook <em>unilateral</em> story driven by diversion. Lead with unilateral effects; flag coordinated risk as the secondary concern.`,
                    coach_r2_underflag:     `<strong>You flagged almost nothing as harmful.</strong> The national sunflower market breaches the screen and three states exceed HHI 3,000 — those are presumptively harmful on the numbers. Under-flagging here is how a real harm slips through to clearance.`,
                    coach_r2_untested:      `<strong>Your concentration table rests on a market you never tested.</strong> HHI computed on the parties' broad market is the wrong HHI. Until the market definition is anchored by a SSNIP test, the screen is measuring the wrong thing — and the NCLAT will say so.`,
                    coach_r2_good:          `<strong>Screen used correctly — as a trigger that shifts the burden, with a unilateral-led theory of harm.</strong> Now the burden sits with the parties on efficiencies and entry, and you have set up the effects estimate the Commission's order will actually rest on.`,
                    coach_r3_lowball:       `<strong>A predicted rise below the evidenced 6–11% band looks like you talked yourself into clearance.</strong> With this diversion and these margins, the UPP/GUPPI estimate sits in the high single digits. Lowballing the effect is how a Type II error gets dressed up as caution.`,
                    coach_r3_overshoot:     `<strong>A double-digit predicted rise overstates the case.</strong> Push diversion too high and the parties' economists will pick the estimate apart on appeal. Keep the diversion in the surveyed 35–45% range so the 6–11% prediction holds up under cross-examination.`,
                    coach_r3_discipline:    `<strong>That is the discipline the efficiencies defence demands.</strong> You credited only the merger-specific, verifiable savings and only the share that passes through to shoppers — not the full slide. The net welfare harm you're left with is one you can defend, because every number behind it survives a test.`,
                    coach_r3_full:          `<strong>You netted the full ₹900 crore against the harm and leaned toward clearing.</strong> But are those savings merger-specific — could Aarav get them by expanding alone? Are they verified, or a slide in the parties' submission? And how much passes through to the shopper versus the shareholder? Credit only what survives all three tests; the rest is advocacy.`,
                    coach_r3_zero:          `<strong>Crediting zero synergies is as undisciplined as crediting all of them.</strong> Some procurement and refining savings may genuinely be merger-specific and partly passed through. The standard is to credit what survives the three-part test — not to refuse the defence on principle.`,
                    coach_r4_structural:    `<strong>A structural divestiture is the clean cure.</strong> Hand the overlapping sunflower brand and a refinery to a credible independent buyer and you restore the rivalry the merger removed — once, monitorably — while the atta business and clean geographies proceed. This is the remedy that survives the appeal.`,
                    coach_r4_behavioural:   `<strong>A price cap sounds clean, but who sets the benchmark and audits it?</strong> Compliance across thousands of SKUs and promotions, for years, with the Commission babysitting the market — a divestiture fixes the structure once. Why prefer the remedy you'll have to police forever?`,
                    coach_r4_block:         `<strong>You prohibited the whole ₹14,200 crore deal.</strong> But the harm is concentrated in sunflower oil and three states — the atta business and most geographies are fine. A targeted divestiture would let the rest proceed. Blocking a mostly-benign merger destroys real synergies and invites a successful appeal. Is prohibition proportionate?`,
                    coach_r4_clear:         `<strong>You cleared a deal your own model says raises prices 6–11% on a staple consumed by hundreds of millions.</strong> That is the Type II error in its purest form — and the cheapest clearance is the most expensive outcome for shoppers. The NCLAT and the public-comment record will not forgive an order that ignored your own estimate.`,
                    coach_r4_best:          `<strong>That is the order the Commission can defend.</strong> A proportionate, structural divestiture in exactly the overlap segment and states where the harm lives — matched to the evidence, restoring competition, letting the benign part of the deal proceed, and delivered inside the clock. It holds up against the efficiencies argument and the appeal.`,

